1) Starting
-----------

Open the Diversions directory and double-click !Pacman. It takes over
the whole screen (or opens in a window, if you chose that last time)
and shows its title. Its icon also appears on the right of the icon
bar, and stays there while you are back at the desktop.

The title shows the four ghosts being introduced one after another,
each with its name and nickname, and what a dot (10) and an energizer
(50) are worth. Then move up and down with the arrow keys (or point
with the mouse) and press Return (or click) to choose:

  Play           start a game
  High scores    the ten best scores (part 5)
  Settings       change the options (part 6)
  How to play    a page of hints and keys
  View source    read the program in !JsEdit (part 9)
  Desktop        leave the game; its icon stays on the icon bar

Escape on the title also goes back to the desktop. On High scores
and How to play, Return or Escape goes back to the title.

Click SELECT on the icon bar icon to come back to the game. Click
MENU on it for:

  Info                  about !Pacman
  Full screen           play on the whole desktop screen
  Window                play in a desktop window, which you can move
                        and resize (click it so that it has the
                        keyboard)
  Sound                 ticked when sound is on; choose it to turn
                        sound off or on
  Browser full screen   make the browser itself full screen too
  View source           read the program in !JsEdit
  Open directory        open the application's directory
  Quit                  leave for good, and take the icon away

Your choice of Full screen or Window (here or in Settings) is
remembered, and the next start opens that kind.
Quitting, even in the middle of a game, leaves nothing behind.


2) Keys
-------

  Arrow keys, or W A S D     steer Pac-Man
  ' (apostrophe), / , Z, X   steer too: up, down, left and right
  Return, Space              choose in a menu
  P                          pause; press it again to go on
  Escape                     in a game, the pause menu; on the
                             title, back to the desktop
  Any key during the demo    back to the title (part 7)
  F                          browser full screen on and off (not
                             while a name is being typed)

A gamepad works too: its pad or left stick steers, A chooses, Start
pauses and B opens the pause menu. In the Settings, left and right
change the option that is chosen.

Pac-Man keeps going in the direction he is facing until a wall stops
him. The last direction you pressed is the one he wants, even after
you let the key go, and he takes it as soon as he can.

Press the turn early. He does not have to reach the middle of a
corner: he may start the turn up to three pixels before it, or still
make it up to three pixels after it, and he then cuts the corner in
a diagonal, moving one pixel along the new way and one pixel towards
its middle line at each step. A turn made early like this gets him
round the corner a few pixels ahead of the same turn made exactly at
the middle, and that is how an experienced player gains a step on
the ghosts, who always turn exactly in the middle of a square. He can
turn straight back the other way at any moment, even half way round
a corner. A turn into a wall cannot be made, but it is not lost: he
goes on, and takes it at the next place where the way is open.

The pause menu has Continue, Restart (a new game, with your lives and
bonus life settings), Settings, Title and Desktop. Continue picks up
exactly where you were. If you click away from the game, or the
browser tab is hidden, the game pauses by itself; press a direction
afterwards and Pac-Man turns that way (the direction you had been
holding is forgotten, so he never sets off on his own).


3) The rules
------------

The maze is the one from the arcade: 244 dots, four of them big
energizers, a tunnel through each side (Pac-Man goes out of one
edge and comes in at the other) and a pen in the middle for the
ghosts.

  A dot is worth 10 points, an energizer 50.

On level 1 the ghosts are a little slower than Pac-Man (75 per cent
of full speed against his 80), and Pac-Man stops for a moment after
every dot he eats (one sixtieth of a second for a dot, three for an
energizer), so keep moving and keep taking the turns early. Every
few levels everyone gets faster: from level 5 Pac-Man runs at full
speed and the ghosts at 95 per cent, so on the later levels you can
no longer outrun them. The ghosts are described in part 4.

The tunnel. Row 14 of the maze leaves the screen at either side and
comes back in at the other. Pac-Man runs through it at his usual
speed, but the ghosts slow right down there: on level 1 they crawl
at 40 per cent of full speed instead of 75 (45, then 50 on later
levels). So if you are being chased, the tunnel is where you gain
ground, and a ghost that follows you in drops back at once. The
slowing starts as the ghost enters the tunnel squares, six at each
side, and stops as it leaves them.

You start with three lives (the Settings can make it 1, 2, 3 or 5).
The spare ones are the little Pac-Men
at the bottom left; one is taken when the game starts, once PLAYER
ONE and READY! have shown for four seconds and the jingle has played.

If a ghost catches you (you are both in the same square of the maze),
everything stops for a second, the ghosts vanish, and Pac-Man folds
up and bursts, which takes about three and a half seconds in all.
Then everyone is put back at the start, a spare life is taken,
READY! shows for two seconds and play goes on, with the dots you
have eaten still gone and your score kept. The fruit is gone and the
scatter and chase clock starts again. When you are caught with no
spare life left, GAME OVER shows for three seconds and you are
back at the title, or asked for your name if you made the top ten
(part 5).

You are given one extra life, once, when your score first reaches
10000. (Settings can make it 15000, 20000 or none.) Eat every
dot and the level is done, and fruit turns up now and then; both are
described below. An energizer turns the tables:

  Eating an energizer turns every ghost blue, even the ones still in
  their pen, and the ones out in the maze turn round at once. For six
  seconds (360 frames) the blue ghosts wander at random, taking any
  turning they choose, and the scatter and chase clock stands still.
  In the last two seconds or so the ghosts flash blue and white as a
  warning, and then they go back to their own colours and the clock
  runs on. Eating a second energizer starts the six seconds again.
  The blue ghosts are slow (half speed on level 1) and Pac-Man
  speeds up a little (90 per cent). From level 2 the time gets
  shorter, down to a second on some levels (and the flashing then
  starts at once), and on others the ghosts do not turn blue at all.

Touch a blue ghost and you eat it. The first ghost of an energizer is
worth 200 points, the second 400, the third 800 and the fourth 1600
(a second energizer starts the count again at 200). The game stops
for a second with Pac-Man out of sight and the points shown where the
ghost was; only ghosts you have already eaten keep moving.

An eaten ghost is just a pair of eyes. It hurries back to the pen
(at twice full speed, far faster than a ghost), goes down through
the door and sideways to its own place, and comes out again as a
ghost of its own colour at once, even if the others are still blue.
Eyes are harmless: you can walk through them.

  Eat all four ghosts on each of the four energizers of a maze, and
  you get a bonus of 12000 points, once.

On the later levels the energizers do less. The blue time shrinks
from six seconds on level 1 to five, four, three, two, then five on
level 6, two on 7 and 8, and a single second on level 9 (with
three flashes instead of five). Level 17 and every level from 19 on
give no blue time at all: an energizer still scores 50 and still
turns every ghost round, but nobody turns blue and nobody can be
eaten.

Fruit. When you have eaten 70 dots a piece of fruit appears just
below the pen, in the middle of the maze, and again when you have
eaten 170. Each stays for about nine or ten seconds (the exact time
varies a little) and goes if you do not take it. Touch it to eat it:
you score its points, shown where it was for two seconds. If you are
caught, the fruit is taken away. It never comes a third time on a
level. The fruit and its points change with the level:

  level 1       cherries       100
  level 2       strawberry     300
  levels 3-4    peach          500
  levels 5-6    apple          700
  levels 7-8    grapes        1000
  levels 9-10   rocket        2000
  levels 11-12  bell          3000
  level 13 on   key           5000

The fruit of the last seven levels is also shown along the bottom of
the screen, to remind you how far you have got.

Finishing a level. Eat the last dot and the game stops. For one
second everything stays still, then the ghosts vanish. After two
seconds in all the maze flashes white four times, and then the next
level begins: every dot is back, everyone is at the start, READY!
shows for two seconds, and you play on with your score. The ghosts
leave the pen sooner (see part 4) and the chases get longer. Levels
go on for ever; from level 21 they stay the same.


4) The ghosts
-------------

Blinky, the red ghost, starts above the pen. The other three wait
in the pen, moving up and down, and come out one after another,
Pinky first, then Inky, then Clyde. They leave by the door at the
top, then turn left.

What lets them out is the dots you eat. Only the ghost next in line
counts them, and it leaves when its own count is reached:

               level 1    level 2    level 3 and later
  Pinky           0          0           0
  Inky           30          0           0
  Clyde          60         50           0

So on level 1 Pinky is out at once, Inky leaves with your 30th dot
and Clyde with the 60th dot after that. Eating an energizer counts
as a dot. A ghost that is on its way out has already been counted,
so the next one starts counting at once.

If you stop eating, the ghosts do not wait for ever: after 4 seconds
without a dot (3 seconds from level 5) the next ghost in line leaves
anyway, and every dot you eat starts those seconds again.

After you lose a life the ghosts all go back into the pen and one
shared count takes over: Pinky leaves after 7 dots, Inky after 17
and Clyde after 32. (If you reach 32 dots while Clyde is still
inside, the shared count is dropped and the personal counts, which
were kept, carry on.) The game waits two seconds with READY! before
you move.

CRUISE ELROY. When there are few dots left, Blinky loses patience.
On level 1, with 20 dots left he speeds up (to 80 per cent of
full speed instead of 75), and with 10 left he speeds up again (85).
He is then called Cruise Elroy, and he goes on chasing you even
when the others are scattering to their corners. Later levels start
this sooner (the table in the program, Levels, has the numbers). If
you lose a life, Elroy is suspended until Clyde has left the pen
again, so a death gives you a little breathing space.

At every junction a ghost looks at the squares it could go to next
and picks the one nearest its target. It never turns back on itself,
and in four places above the pen and four near the bottom of the
maze it may not turn upwards (blue ghosts and eyes may). So you can
read where a ghost will go, and lead it a dance. Each has its own
idea of a target:

  Blinky (red)    goes straight for Pac-Man's square.
  Pinky (pink)    aims four squares ahead of Pac-Man. When Pac-Man
                  faces up she aims four up and four left too, as
                  the arcade ghost did by mistake.
  Inky (blue)     looks at the square two ahead of Pac-Man and
                  at Blinky; his target is as far beyond that
                  square as Blinky is on this side of it. Inky is
                  most dangerous when Blinky is close to you.
  Clyde (orange)  chases Pac-Man until he is within eight squares,
                  then loses his nerve and goes for his own corner.

The ghosts also take turns. They start in SCATTER: for 7 seconds
each heads for a corner outside the maze (Blinky top right, Pinky
top left, Inky bottom right, Clyde bottom left) and so circles round
it. Then they CHASE you with the targets above for 20 seconds, then
scatter for 7, chase for 20, scatter for 5, chase for 20, scatter
for 5, and after that chase for ever. Every time the mode changes
all the ghosts in the maze turn right round, which is your warning.
On later levels the chases get longer.


5) Scores and high scores
-------------------------

Your score is at the top left, under 1UP. HIGH SCORE in the middle
is the best score in the table, or your own score as soon as it is
higher. Points are 10 for a dot, 50 for an energizer, 200, 400, 800
and 1600 for ghosts in a row (and 12000 more for eating all four
ghosts at all four energizers of a level), and the fruit, whose
value goes up with the level.

The table
  Choose High scores on the title. It lists the ten best scores,
  best first, each with its rank, a name of three characters, the
  score, the level you reached and the date. A new copy of the game
  starts with ten scores of our own (ARM at 10000 down to POW at
  1000) for you to beat. Return or Escape goes back to the title.

Entering your name
  When GAME OVER has shown, a score of more than nothing that gets
  into the top ten asks for your name. A score that does not (zero,
  or no better than tenth place) goes straight back to the title.
  Type up to three characters: letters (shown as capitals), digits,
  space, full stop and minus. Backspace takes the last one off. Any
  other key does nothing, and a fourth character is ignored. A key
  you hold down does not repeat letters. F and P are letters here,
  not browser full screen and pause. The name you typed last time
  is already there, ready to keep or change.
  Return or Escape finishes. If you typed nothing, the name is
  ??? . The table is saved at once, and the High scores screen
  shows where you came.

Where the table is kept
  In Choices:Pacman.Scores (part 8). A file that is missing or
  damaged gives the starting table; an over-long one is cut to ten
  and odd characters in names and dates are removed.

6) Sound and settings
---------------------

All the sounds are made by the program itself, when you first click
or press a key (browsers only allow sound after that): a tune of our
own plays as a game begins, each dot you eat gives a short "waka",
alternating between two tones, and there are sounds for eating a
ghost, eating fruit, an extra life and losing a life.

While you play there is always one background sound. A siren goes
round the maze, and it steps up in pitch five times as the dots run
out. When an energizer turns the ghosts blue the siren gives way to
a low warbling; and while any eaten ghost's eyes are hurrying home,
you hear a bright, falling whistle over everything. Pausing, losing
focus, GAME OVER and the title are silent, and so is the demo.

Sound on the icon bar menu
  MENU on the icon bar icon has a Sound item with a tick when sound
  is on. Choose it to turn the sound off (the tick goes) or on
  again. The choice is remembered.

Volume
  How loud it is depends on two things multiplied together: the
  game's own volume (20 to 100 per cent, 80 to start with) and the
  desktop's volume. Turn the desktop's volume down in the Configure
  window, or its speaker off, and the game goes quiet with it.

The Settings screen
  Choose Settings on the title or on the pause menu. Up and down
  choose an option; left and right (or Return, or a click) change
  it, and the change takes effect at once and is saved. Escape, or
  Back, returns to where you came from; from the pause menu the game
  is still waiting for you.

  Display      Full screen or Window. Changing it moves the game
               there and now, and the next start opens that kind.
  Browser      In page or Full screen: also make the browser
               itself full screen (F does the same).
               Leaving full screen with the browser's own Escape
               is remembered too.
  Sound        On or Off (the icon bar menu's Sound item too).
  Volume       20%, 40%, 60%, 80% (to start with) or 100%.
  Lives        1, 2, 3 (to start with) or 5, for the next game.
  Bonus life   the score that gives the extra life: 10000 (to
               start with), 15000, 20000 or None.

Lives and Bonus life count from the next game you start (Play or
Restart); a game already going on keeps its own.

All of these are kept in Choices:Pacman.Settings (the high scores
are in Choices:Pacman.Scores, part 5). If the file is
damaged, or a value in it is not one of the above, that setting goes
back to the standard one.


7) The demo
-----------

Leave the title screen alone for ten seconds, with no key pressed
and no click, and the game plays itself: this is the demo, as on an
arcade machine. Pac-Man is steered by the computer, who runs from
ghosts, chases blue ones and heads for the nearest dots. It is
silent, and the maze says DEMO where a game would say PLAYER ONE and
GAME OVER (READY! shows for a moment at the start, as in a game).

The demo is over when Pac-Man loses his first life or after a
minute, whichever comes first. The table of high scores then shows
for six seconds, and then the title comes back, and ten seconds
later another demo starts. Each demo is a different game.

Any key, or a click, during the demo or the table that follows takes
you straight back to the title. That key or click is used up in
doing so: it does not choose anything in the menu, so Return does
not start a game by accident. Press Return again to play.

The ten seconds count only on the title itself. They start again
after any key or click, and they do not run while you are in
Settings, How to play, High scores, a game, the pause menu or typing
a name.

Nothing the demo scores is ever put in the table of high scores,
however high it goes, and it never asks for a name.


8) Where things are kept
------------------------

!Pacman remembers whether you play full screen or in a window, and
browser full screen, in Choices:Pacman, which on the hard disc is
$.!Boot.Choices.Pacman:

  Settings   your choices (part 6)
  Scores     the high score table and the name you typed last

Each is a text file (JSON) that you can read in !Edit. Delete
Settings to go back to the standard settings, or Scores to go back
to the starting table of high scores (that loses your own scores);
a damaged file is ignored and the standard one is used instead.


9) Reading and changing the program
-----------------------------------

All of !Pacman is written in JavaScript, and every part of it is in
the application directory, with notes for readers at the top of each
file. To read it, choose View source on the title or the icon bar
menu: !JsEdit opens the directory in a directory view, and you can
double-click a file to read it. From the Filer, Shift-double-click
!Pacman to open the directory, then Shift-double-click a file to open
it in !JsEdit (a plain double-click would try to run it).

The files, and what each one does:

  !RunImage   where it starts: the icon bar icon, the screen or
              window, the keys, and the main loop, which moves the
              game on 60 times a second and draws each picture
  Dirs        up, left, down, right, as numbers
  MazeData    the maze, as a list of rows of text
  Theme       the title, the ghosts' names and all the colours
  Maze        walls, dots and energizers; which ways are open
  Mover       how many pixels something moves each frame
  Player      how Pac-Man moves and eats
  Ghost       a ghost: bobbing in the pen, leaving it, moving
              through the maze
  Modes       the scatter and chase clock
  Fright      how long the ghosts stay blue, and when they flash
  Fruit       when the bonus fruit appears and how long it stays
  Levels      the table of what changes on each level
  Targets     where a ghost is heading and how it picks a way
  Play        one frame of play, in the arcade's order
  Game        the state of a game, and its clock
  MazeDraw    draws the walls
  Sprites     Pac-Man and the ghosts
  FruitArt    the fruit
  Hud         the score panel
  Render      draws a whole picture of the game
  Menu        the menus on the title and pause screens
  Title       the title screen
  Screens     which screen is showing, and what the keys do on it

The rules and the drawing are kept apart from the desktop: only
!RunImage talks to it. The parts the game shares with other games
(main loop, drawing, text, keys, saved files) are in GameLib; its
guide lists them.

Changing it

Before you change anything, make a copy to work on: drag !Pacman from
the Diversions directory into a directory of your own (on the hard
disc, say), and change the copy. The original stays as it was.

Here are two small changes to try. In your copy, open Theme in
!JsEdit. In the list of COLOURS near the top, the line

  pac: [255, 255, 0],

gives Pac-Man's colour as red, green and blue parts from 0 to 255:
change it to [255, 128, 0] for an orange Pac-Man. Or open Play and
open Levels and find the first line of the table, level 1:

  [1, 1, 80, 90, 75, 40, 50, 20, 80, 10, 85, 6, 5],

After the two 1s, which say which levels the line is for, come the
speeds of Pac-Man (80), Pac-Man when the ghosts are blue (90), the
ghosts (75), the ghosts in the tunnel (40) and blue ghosts (50), as
percentages of full speed. Try 60 for the ghosts to make them easier
to escape, or 90 to make them a real danger.

To try the change, quit !Pacman if it is running (Quit on its icon
bar menu), then double-click your copy: the program is read afresh
each time it starts. If you make a mistake, an error box says which
file and line it is in: open that file, put it right and try again.


10) Credits
-----------

A personal, non-commercial tribute to Namco's 1980 arcade game.
Pac-Man is a trademark of Bandai Namco; this program is not connected
with them.

!Pacman is new code, written from descriptions of how the arcade game
behaves; it contains nothing from the original program or its data.
The pictures are drawn by the program itself, and the sounds are made
by GameLib's synthesiser from simple waveforms of our own, with tunes
of our own.
